# inc-19ay, scenario 1 of 2: a breather that declares its own dice and carries
# no template, so Power is 0 and the declared dice are the whole answer.
#
# THE MONSTER IS A HELL HOUND, NOT A DRAKE, and the file keeps its old name
# only because check_breath_dice.sh names its two scenarios "drake" and
# "dragon". The flame drake this was written for CANNOT BE SUMMONED AT ALL:
# its only declared MType is MA_REPTILIAN (lib/mon1.irh:1474), MA_REPTILIAN is
# 83 (inc/Defines.h:1618), and TextTerm::MonsterTypePrompt (src/Term.cpp:2887)
# offers only types up to MA_LAST_REAL, which is 74. Nothing in
# Creature::isMType puts a flame drake into a type below that line, so it is on
# neither page of the summon menu. Every drake is MA_REPTILIAN, so the same is
# true of all four.
#
# The hell hound declares A_BREA for 2d6 AD_FIRE (lib/mon4.irh:1999), which is
# the same 2 dice the drake declared, and it is an MA_DOG, so the summon menu
# lists it under Dogs.
#
# INCURSION_BREATH_PROBE records the actual dice before damage is delivered.
@include chargen.keys

# chargen.keys ends in play mode, so the first key here is a game command and
# NOT a prompt dismissal: an ESC opens the System Menu (src/Player.cpp:845) and
# every later keystroke then reads the wrong screen. Go straight to wizard
# mode, the way kobold-horn-hostile.keys does. The first wizard entry asks for
# confirmation; enable Cheat Death (the first switch) so a level-one character
# can give every summoned monster a turn.
w
y
@choose "Wizard Mode Switches"
SPACE
ESC

# Place it three squares east, in sight and close enough to breathe.
w
@choose "Monster Summoning"
@choose "Dogs"
@choose "hell hound"
RIGHT*3
ENTER
@dump:hound-summoned

# NO "All Monster Aggravate" HERE. That wizard entry crashes the game:
# src/Debug.cpp:1005 passes myHandle -- an object handle -- as the rID
# argument of GainPermStati, Thing::GainPermStati asserts theGame->Get(eID)
# on it (src/Status.cpp:188), and the session then segfaults. A monster the
# wizard menu summons is hostile already, so the aggravate was never needed.

# Each dot gives the monsters a turn. In Player::Death, n declines death
# and restores HP; y accepts death and can prevent the next breath.
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait01
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait02
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait03
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait04
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait05
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait06
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait07
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait08
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait09
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait10
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait11
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait12
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait13
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait14
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait15
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait16

@quit
